---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
import balajiPhoto from '../assets/balaji.jpg';
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="Software Architecture. Artificial Intelligence. Continuous Learning."
  pubDate={new Date()}
>
  <p class="tagline">Software Architecture · Artificial Intelligence · Continuous Learning</p>

  <Image
    src={balajiPhoto}
    alt="Balaji Bankapur"
    width={400}
    class="about-photo"
    loading="eager"
  />

  <p>
    I’m <strong>Balaji Bankapur</strong>, a Senior Principal Software Engineer
    and Software Architect with more than 20 years of experience building
    software products and complex engineering systems — spanning operating
    systems, enterprise software, healthcare technology, and medical devices.
    My work has increasingly focused on software architecture and system
    design, where reliability, safety, and long-lived engineering discipline
    really matter.
  </p>

  <p>
    Today, I’m exploring how Artificial Intelligence is reshaping the way we
    design and build software — from the perspective of an engineer and
    architect, not a spectator.
  </p>

  <h2>Background</h2>

  <p>
    My career started with traditional software engineering, and over time
    moved from writing software to thinking about how entire systems should
    be designed: how components interact, how information flows, how failures
    are handled, and how architectural decisions age over years of production
    use. Working on medical-device and healthcare software reinforced a belief
    I carry into everything I build:
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    Good technology is not just about making something work. It’s about making
    it reliable, understandable, maintainable, and fit for its purpose.
  </blockquote>

  <p>That architectural mindset now shapes how I look at AI.</p>

  <h2>Where I’m Focused</h2>

  <p>
    Large Language Models are no longer just tools for generating text —
    they’re becoming components inside software systems that reason, retrieve
    knowledge, use tools, and coordinate with other agents. That raises real
    architectural questions: how to design AI systems that are reliable,
    evaluable, and worth trusting in production. Those are the questions I’m
    working through here.
  </p>

  <p>My current focus areas:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Generative AI</strong> — LLMs, RAG, vector databases, prompt engineering, evaluation, and AI application architecture.</li>
    <li><strong>Agentic AI</strong> — planning, tool use, multi-agent systems, orchestration, and autonomous workflows.</li>
    <li><strong>AI + Software Architecture</strong> — patterns for integrating LLMs, agents, knowledge stores, memory, and evaluators into real systems.</li>
    <li><strong>Knowledge &amp; Reasoning</strong> — knowledge graphs, semantic relationships, AI memory, and how machines represent and retrieve what they know.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How I Write</h2>

  <p>
    I believe the best way to understand technology is to build with it. Reading
    about an AI architecture and actually implementing one are two very
    different things — the second is where the honest questions live: what
    happens when the model is wrong, when an agent gets stuck, when cost or
    latency blow up, when evaluation is harder than the feature itself.
  </p>

  <p>
    This site is where I document those experiments, decisions, successes, and
    failures. You’ll find articles on practical AI, agentic systems, software
    architecture, and hands-on prototypes — including the ones that didn’t
    work, because a failed experiment often teaches more than a polished demo.
    I try to write for engineers and architects who want more signal than hype.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond the Code</h2>

  <p>
    Outside of technology, I’m interested in how people think, learn, remember,
    and make decisions — psychology, cognitive science, and human learning.
    As AI systems become more capable, the relationship between human and
    machine intelligence feels increasingly worth understanding.
  </p>

  <h2>Get in Touch</h2>

  <p>
    If you’re working on practical AI, AI architecture, or the challenges of
    taking AI from prototype to production, I’d enjoy the conversation. You
    can reach me at
    <a href="mailto:bankapur.balaji@gmail.com">bankapur.balaji@gmail.com</a>.
  </p>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  .tagline {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: rgb(var(--gray));
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    margin: -0.5rem 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .about-photo {
    width: 220px;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 8px;
    float: right;
    margin: 0.25rem 0 1rem 1.5rem;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.12);
    shape-outside: margin-box;
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .about-photo {
      float: none;
      display: block;
      margin: 0 auto 1.5rem;
      width: 200px;
    }
  }
  article blockquote {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    padding: 0.25rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.05em;
  }
  article h2 { margin-top: 2.25rem; }
</style>
